---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Lesson 9: Basic Sensors and Input Devices - C00lnerd Tutoring">
    <div style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem;">
        
            <!-- Header -->
            <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem; border-radius: 15px; margin-bottom: 2rem; text-align: center;">
                <h1 style="font-size: 2.5rem; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 1rem 0;">Lesson 9: Basic Sensors and Input Devices</h1>
                <p style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0; opacity: 0.9;">Explore the world of sensors and digital/analog inputs</p>
                <div class="mt-4 p-4 bg-blue-100 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="text-blue-800 font-medium">🔍 From hardware to sensing the world around us</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Lesson 8 Recap: From Sensor Values to Actions -->
            <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-lg p-8 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-800 mb-6">🔁 Lesson 8 Recap: From Sensor Values to Actions</h2>

                <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                    In Lesson 8, you learned the core pattern used in real Arduino projects: <strong>read → decide → act</strong>.
                    Lesson 9 builds on that by adding <strong>new kinds of inputs</strong> (digital buttons and analog knobs) and combining
                    them into a single program.
                </p>

                <div style="background: #e0f2fe; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #0284c7; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                    <h4 style="color: #075985; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">What you used in Lesson 8</h4>
                    <p style="color: #075985; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                        You practiced reading sensor values with <strong>analogRead()</strong>, comparing them to <strong>thresholds</strong>, organizing code with <strong>functions</strong>,
                        and even smoothing readings by taking an <strong>average</strong>.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.8; color: #4a5568;">
                    <li><strong>Thresholds:</strong> choosing a value that changes behavior (like LED on/off)</li>
                    <li><strong>Levels:</strong> low / medium / high responses using <strong>if / else if</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>Averaging:</strong> taking multiple readings to reduce noise</li>
                    <li><strong>Functions:</strong> keeping sensor logic organized and reusable</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #eff6ff; border-left: 4px solid #2563eb; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 2rem; color: #1e3a8a;">
                Use Wokwi as your default workspace for these sensor and input experiments so you can debug readings and Serial Monitor output before switching to physical components.
            </div>

            <!-- Learning Objectives -->
            <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-lg p-8 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-800 mb-6">📚 Learning Objectives</h2>
                <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
                    <div>
                        <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem; display: flex; align-items: center;">By the end of this lesson, you will:</h3>
                        <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; display: flex; align-items: flex-start;">
                                <span class="text-blue-600 mr-2">•</span>
                                Understand different types of sensors and their applications
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; display: flex; align-items: flex-start;">
                                <span class="text-blue-600 mr-2">•</span>
                                Use an ultrasonic distance sensor (HC-SR04) to measure distance
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; display: flex; align-items: flex-start;">
                                <span class="text-blue-600 mr-2">•</span>
                                Implement button inputs with proper debouncing
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; display: flex; align-items: flex-start;">
                                <span class="text-blue-600 mr-2">•</span>
                                Use a potentiometer as a live "threshold knob" (setpoint)
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem; display: flex; align-items: center;">Key Concepts:</h3>
                        <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; display: flex; align-items: flex-start;">
                                <span class="text-blue-600 mr-2">•</span>
                                Time-of-flight distance measurement
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; display: flex; align-items: flex-start;">
                                <span class="text-blue-600 mr-2">•</span>
                                Digital inputs with pull-ups and debouncing
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; display: flex; align-items: flex-start;">
                                <span class="text-blue-600 mr-2">•</span>
                                Thresholds, levels, and smoothing (averaging)
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; display: flex; align-items: flex-start;">
                                <span class="text-blue-600 mr-2">•</span>
                                Mapping analog values to useful ranges (setpoints)
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
